  "live_records": {
    "q-334931": {
      "dom_id": "step-q334931",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x-4{t}^{3})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x-4{t}^{3})^{2}] & =2(x-4{t}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(x-4{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2(x-4{t}^{3})\\cdot (-12{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =-24{t}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}-24{t}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-24{t}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{2}-4{t}^{3}x{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2-8{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & =-24{t}^{2}(2-8{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =48{t}^{2}(4{t}^{3}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
